// Daylight you can name.
//
// (Not 'skies' -- POV-Ray's own skies.inc converts to a library of that name, and it holds cloud
// pigments rather than skies to stand under.)
//
// The physical sky in this ray tracer is a real one: it works out the color of every part of the dome
// from the way air scatters sunlight, given where the sun stands and how hazy the day is.  That is
// what makes it look right, and it is also what makes it hard to use, because the numbers it wants
// are the ones a person is least able to guess.  Nobody knows what turbidity they want.  Everybody
// knows what a clear morning looks like.
//
// So this library holds skies by name.  Each is a sky and the light that goes with it, and the two
// are meant to be taken together -- a scene that takes one without the other gets a picture that
// disagrees with itself, since a sky light is what replaces the flat ambient guess that stands in for
// bounced light when there is nothing better.  Take both:
//
//     import 'daylight' { ClearMorning, ClearMorningLight }
//
//     background ClearMorning
//     light ClearMorningLight
//
// Every one of these may be adjusted where it is used, so the names are a starting point rather than
// a straitjacket.  A scene wanting the same morning with less grain in the shadows says:
//
//     light ClearMorningLight { samples 64 }
//
// **On the two numbers that decide a sky.**  Elevation is how high the sun stands, in degrees above
// the horizon: 0 is sunrise, 90 is directly overhead, and the interesting light is all below about 30.
// Azimuth is which way it lies, in degrees, and matters only in relation to where the camera looks --
// change it and the shadows swing round.  Turbidity is how much haze the air holds: 2 is a clear day
// after rain, 3 is ordinary, 6 or more is a hot afternoon in a city, and higher makes the sun's own
// glow spread wider and the blue wash out.
//
// The lights are given a modest number of samples, which is what a sky light works by: it looks at the
// dome from many directions at each point and asks what it can see.  More samples means less grain and
// more time.  Twenty-four is enough for a picture to look at; a large or shadowed scene may want more.

// -- Clear days ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Mid-morning, the sun well up and the air washed clean.  The workhorse: bright, blue, and unfussy.
ClearMorning = pigment physical sky { sun elevation 38  sun azimuth 110  turbidity 2.2  brightness 3 }
ClearMorningLight = sky light { samples 24 }

// The sun overhead and nothing in the way.  Shadows fall almost straight down, which is unflattering
// to most things and exactly right for heat.
ClearNoon = pigment physical sky { sun elevation 78  sun azimuth 180  turbidity 2.4  brightness 3 }
ClearNoonLight = sky light { samples 24 }

// Late afternoon, an hour or two before the sun goes.  Long shadows and a warmth in the light that no
// amount of choosing colors by hand gets right.
GoldenHour = pigment physical sky { sun elevation 9  sun azimuth 255  turbidity 2.8  brightness 3 }
GoldenHourLight = sky light { samples 32 }

// The sun on the horizon.  Almost all the light is red by the time it arrives, having crossed the
// whole thickness of the air to get here.
Sunset = pigment physical sky { sun elevation 1.5  sun azimuth 268  turbidity 3.2  brightness 3 }
SunsetLight = sky light { samples 40 }

// Mid to late afternoon, the sun well down but nowhere near going, with a little haze in the air.
// This is the commonest daylight there is, and the library did not have it: it jumped from `ClearNoon`
// at seventy-eight degrees straight to `GoldenHour` at nine, and every scene that wanted an ordinary
// afternoon wrote its own sky rather than reach in here.  These two came out of three scenes that had
// each done exactly that, which is how a library is supposed to find out what it is short of.  Their
// *elevation and turbidity* are those scenes'; the azimuth here is only the house default, since anything
// that cares gives its own through the companions at the foot of this file.
ClearAfternoon = pigment physical sky { sun elevation 34  sun azimuth 205  turbidity 3  brightness 3 }
ClearAfternoonLight = sky light { samples 32 }

// The same hour with more in the air -- not the flat white of `HazyAfternoon`, just a softer edge to
// every shadow and a sun a few degrees lower.
SoftAfternoon = pigment physical sky { sun elevation 30  sun azimuth 215  turbidity 3.4  brightness 3 }
SoftAfternoonLight = sky light { samples 32 }

// Late afternoon proper: the sun well down and the shadows long, but an hour or so short of turning
// gold.  The library jumped from thirty degrees straight to nine, and thirty is barely more raking than
// noon while nine throws a shadow six times an object's height across everything behind it.  Twenty is
// the hour a scene usually means by "raking".
//
// **It carries more `brightness` than the others**, 3.5 against 3, and that is not a thumb on the scale:
// a sun twenty degrees up delivers less light than one at thirty-four, measurably so -- the same scene
// lost a fifth of its mean brightness moving between them -- and opening up to compensate is what anyone
// photographing at that hour does.
LateAfternoon = pigment physical sky { sun elevation 20  sun azimuth 212  turbidity 2.9  brightness 3.5 }
LateAfternoonLight = sky light { samples 32 }

// -- Hazier days -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A hot afternoon in summer, the air thick enough to soften every edge and pale the blue overhead.
HazyAfternoon = pigment physical sky { sun elevation 46  sun azimuth 220  turbidity 6  brightness 3 }
HazyAfternoonLight = sky light { samples 32 }

// A low winter sun through cold, still air.  Bright without being warm, and the shadows go on forever.
WinterSun = pigment physical sky { sun elevation 14  sun azimuth 160  turbidity 2  brightness 3 }
WinterSunLight = sky light { samples 32 }

// -- No sun in sight -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// An overcast day, made rather than derived: a real sky with the sun taken out of it is not the same
// thing as a cloudy one, since cloud spreads the sun's light across the whole dome instead of hiding
// it.  So this is a plain gradient, cool overhead and paler at the horizon, and a light gathered from
// all of it.  Shadows are soft to the point of vanishing, which is what an overcast day looks like.
OvercastHigh = color [0.42, 0.46, 0.54]
OvercastLow = color [0.62, 0.64, 0.68]
Overcast = pigment linear gradient {
    [0, OvercastLow, 1, OvercastHigh]
    rotate Z 90
    scale 2
    translate Y -1
}
OvercastLight = sky light { samples 48 }

// Twilight after the sun has gone, when what light there is comes from the whole sky at once.
DuskHigh = color [0.05, 0.06, 0.11]
DuskLow = color [0.16, 0.19, 0.27]
Dusk = pigment linear gradient {
    [0, DuskLow, 1, DuskHigh]
    rotate Z 90
    scale 2
    translate Y -1
}
DuskLight = sky light { samples 48  color [0.9, 0.92, 1] }

// -- Choosing where the sun stands -----------------------------------------------------------------

// Every sky above names an azimuth as well as an elevation, and that is the one number in a sky that
// is **not** a property of the weather.  Elevation is the hour, turbidity is the air, brightness is the
// exposure -- but azimuth is a property of the *composition*: which way the subject faces and where the
// camera stands.  Welding it into a named sky means the name only serves scenes that happen to face the
// right way.
//
// **And none of the ones above face the usual way.**  The sky works its direction to the sun out as
//
//     (cos(elevation) * sin(azimuth), sin(elevation), -cos(elevation) * cos(azimuth))
//
// so the Z part is negative only for an azimuth within ninety degrees of nought.  Every stock sky above
// was written with an azimuth outside that, which means every one of them lights the **far** side of its
// subject -- and the buildings in this collection face -Z while the vehicles and trains face +X, both
// seen from a camera standing at -Z.  A scene using one of these names straight got a backlit subject,
// so scene after scene wrote its own sky instead, and the library never grew.
//
// So each sky has a companion that takes the azimuth and keeps everything else:
//
//     background ClearAfternoonFrom(40)
//     sky light { samples 32 }
//
// **Which azimuth?**  Point it at the face you want lit and then swing it thirty or forty degrees off,
// so the light rakes rather than flattens.  For a subject facing -Z, that is an azimuth around 30 to 50
// -- and for one facing +X, subtract ninety.  A sun directly behind the camera lights everything and
// models nothing.
//
// `Overcast` and `Dusk` have no companions here because they have no sun in them; there is nothing for
// an azimuth to mean.

primitive ClearMorningFrom(azimuth) -> pigment {
    return physical sky { sun elevation 38  sun azimuth azimuth  turbidity 2.2  brightness 3 }
}

primitive ClearNoonFrom(azimuth) -> pigment {
    return physical sky { sun elevation 78  sun azimuth azimuth  turbidity 2.4  brightness 3 }
}

primitive ClearAfternoonFrom(azimuth) -> pigment {
    return physical sky { sun elevation 34  sun azimuth azimuth  turbidity 3  brightness 3 }
}

primitive SoftAfternoonFrom(azimuth) -> pigment {
    return physical sky { sun elevation 30  sun azimuth azimuth  turbidity 3.4  brightness 3 }
}

primitive GoldenHourFrom(azimuth) -> pigment {
    return physical sky { sun elevation 9  sun azimuth azimuth  turbidity 2.8  brightness 3 }
}

primitive SunsetFrom(azimuth) -> pigment {
    return physical sky { sun elevation 1.5  sun azimuth azimuth  turbidity 3.2  brightness 3 }
}

primitive HazyAfternoonFrom(azimuth) -> pigment {
    return physical sky { sun elevation 46  sun azimuth azimuth  turbidity 6  brightness 3 }
}

primitive WinterSunFrom(azimuth) -> pigment {
    return physical sky { sun elevation 14  sun azimuth azimuth  turbidity 2  brightness 3 }
}

primitive LateAfternoonFrom(azimuth) -> pigment {
    return physical sky { sun elevation 20  sun azimuth azimuth  turbidity 2.9  brightness 3.5 }
}
